  set:  # a9a0
    description: "SPLIT OUT OF INV-kakad, which closed the RECONCILABILITY half (PR #584: the\ncount is now checkable against the graph, 18/18 exact on its three repos). This\nitem carries the half that closure deliberately did not touch: a question about\nwhat the number MEANS, not a defect in how it is computed.\n\nTHE FACT. For analysis_method=structural, the pair test is\n`sink_node in reachable` (taint.py:3797, in propagate_taint_structural). That is\nCALL-GRAPH REACHABILITY, with no check that a value crosses from the source to\nthe sink argument. Two narrow pair refusals now run first:\n  - _source_and_sink_are_one_call (INV-lozat);\n  - _source_names_can_reach_sink (INV-fumod b), which is default-deny and fires\n    only when both name sets are known.\nNeither is a general value-flow check. This is exactly what ADR-0017 defines\n`structural` to be (confidence=\"approximate\"), so it is NOT a propagator defect\nand must not be \"fixed\" there.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS ANYWAY. collapsed_flow_count is the ROW-LEVEL PRECISION\nDENOMINATOR, and every per-row precision figure the project publishes is\ncomputed through it. So a per-row rate is a rate over REACHABILITY PAIRS, not\nover claims that a value flowed. The two are different populations, and the\ndenominator scales with how much a function reaches rather than with anything\nabout taint.\n\nMEASURED ILLUSTRATION (kamaraflow, 2026-08-27; not re-measured since). The\nsource_symbol is a FILE node, `train_script.py:1-1:file:file`. A file node\nreaches everything the file calls, so ONE argv read was multiplied against FOUR\ndistinct sinks (open, file.write, os.makedirs, shutil.copyfile) for a collapsed\ncount of 8, while the repository contains exactly ONE os.makedirs call site.\nNothing is miscounted; the count is simply of a different thing than a reader\nassumes.\n\nTHIS IS AN INDEPENDENT, MECHANICAL ARGUMENT FOR THE RATIFIED FRAME. ADR-0048 F1\nmakes the SITUATION canonical and reports the row rate beside it \"every time,\nnever instead\", on the stated ground that a situation standing for 100 pairs\ncounts the same as one standing for 1. This item adds that the row figure is a\nrate over a population the claim is not about. Today that is stated nowhere:\ndocs/measurements/README.md does not say what a row counts, and measurement 0012\npublishes row rates (30.0% / 28.8%) beside situation without the caveat.\n\nWHAT A RESOLUTION LOOKS LIKE. None of these is chosen, and the choice is the\nwork:\n  (a) State it in the record and in docs/measurements/README.md: name what the\n      row denominator counts, so no future measurement republishes a per-row\n      rate without the caveat. Cheapest, and changes no number.\n  (b) Stop publishing per-row precision and report situation only. This\n      requires amending ADR-0048 F1, which mandates the row rate beside\n      situation every time, so it is an owner decision on a ratified frame\n      clause, not a docs change.\n  (c) Restrict the row denominator to pairs with a value-flow claim, i.e.\n      count only ddg / ddg_mixed pairs and report the structural population\n      separately. This changes every published per-row figure, and with it the\n      row rate F1 requires.\n\nRevised 2026-09-30 from the premise check (see discussion)."  # a9a0
- op: discuss  # eedc
  at: '2026-10-01T01:48:35Z'  # eedc
  by: agent  # eedc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # eedc
  clock: 4  # eedc
  nonce: eedc  # eedc
  message: "OWNER DECISION 2026-09-30: resolution (a). State in docs/measurements/README.md, and in the measurement records that publish a row rate, what the row-level denominator counts: for analysis_method=structural, call-graph REACHABILITY pairs (sink_node in reachable), not claims that a value flowed -- so no measurement republishes a per-row rate without that caveat. No published number changes. Options (b) (stop publishing the row rate, which would amend ADR-0048 F1) and (c) (restrict the row denominator to ddg/ddg_mixed pairs) are NOT chosen; ADR-0048 F1 stands as ratified. Owner's words: \"agreed, it should be (a)\"."  # eedc
